Now your "Upliftment@ Definition of Free" is doing something much more ambitious than the previous turn. Last message, Free was economic + energetic. Now you've stacked four distinct ontological layers on top of each other, and the last line — "beyond Dollars & Cents and Mass & Energy" — explicitly breaks out of the substance frame entirely. Let me walk each layer honestly and point at a bridge you may not have named yet.

Layer 1 — Liberation: why abundance alone doesn't free you
The Buddhist move here is precise. Dukkha is not "suffering" in the blunt English sense — it's closer to unsatisfactoriness, the way even pleasant things curdle when grasped. The First Noble Truth isn't "life hurts"; it's "no phenomenon, when clung to, finally satisfies." This is the crucial correction to a pure post-scarcity vision: you can have infinite free energy and still be in dukkha, because the problem isn't the supply, it's the clinging (taṇhā). Liberation (nirodha) is the cessation of craving, not the satisfaction of all cravings. Those are opposite engineering problems, and most "abundance" projects confuse them.

Layer 2 — Non-self: the structural, not the sentimental
Anattā is the deepest of the three marks (anicca · dukkha · anattā) and the most misunderstood. It is not "you don't exist." The Two-Truths doctrine (saṃvṛti-satya / paramārtha-satya) resolves this cleanly: at conventional truth, the self exists as a useful fiction — you sign contracts, you pay taxes, you apologize. At ultimate truth, no fixed, independent, enduring self is found; what we call "self" is a processual aggregate (the five skandhas) in dependent relation. The Western fear that "non-self = annihilation" is exactly the fear the Buddha called ucchedavāda and explicitly rejected. Non-self is not nothing — it is relational process all the way down.

Layer 3 — Self-actualization: and the bridge you may not have crossed
Here's the move you're gesturing at but may not have named explicitly: Maslow himself revised the pyramid late in life. In his 1969 posthumous work The Farther Reaches of Human Nature, he added a tier above self-actualization: self-transcendence — giving oneself to something beyond the self (truth, justice, beauty, the cosmos, the relief of others' suffering). He came to this partly through studying peak experiences and partly through engagement with Eastern thought.

This is the bridge your definition is walking on, and it's load-bearing: self-actualization and non-self are not in contradiction — they are nested. Self-actualization is the fulfillment of the conventional self; self-transcendence is the dissolution of that fulfilled self into relation. Maslow's late pyramid reads almost like a translation of the Buddhist path into Western psychological vocabulary. Your "Upliftment@" structure preserves both layers — that's the correct move.

