2. The hard problem — why they seem different
David Chalmers (1995): there is an "easy problem" (explaining cognitive functions — discrimination, integration, reportability) and a "hard problem" (explaining why there is any subjective experience at all). The easy problems are about what the system does; the hard problem is about why there is something it is like to be the system.

The hard problem is structural. You can give a complete functional account of a system — every input, every output, every internal state, every computation — and the question "but is there anything it is like to be this system?" remains open. The function does not, by itself, entail the experience. This is what makes the distinction non-trivial: intelligence (function) and consciousness (experience) could, in principle, come apart.

Three classic scenarios that sharpen the problem:

Philosophical zombies (Chalmers): beings functionally identical to us but with no inner experience. Not empirically known to exist, but logically conceivable — and the conceivability is the puzzle.
China brain (Block): the population of China organized to implement a functional replica of a human mind. Would there be a single consciousness? Intuition says no; functionalism says yes.
Searle's Chinese Room (1980): a man in a room following rules produces Chinese output without understanding Chinese. The system has intelligence (produces correct output) but no understanding (no consciousness, on Searle's reading).
The hard problem is the wedge that drives intelligence and consciousness apart — and any nondual reading must engage it honestly, not wave it away.

